    <div class="section">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>Vanadium is a hard, silvery-gray, ductile transition metal. It forms a protective oxide layer that makes it corrosion-resistant. Vanadium is named after Vanadis, the Scandinavian goddess of beauty, because of its beautiful multicolored compounds.</p>
        <p>Vanadium was discovered twice: first in 1801 by Andrés Manuel del Río who called it "erythronium," and independently in 1830 by Nils Gabriel Sefström who named it vanadium. The element was isolated in 1867 by Sir Henry Enfield Roscoe. Most vanadium is used as an alloying agent in steel.</p>
    </div>

